[Repertoire "black-caro"]
[Side "black"]
[Title "The Caro-Kann"]
[Tagline "Solid, reliable, and your light-squared bishop gets out before you lock the center."]
[Chapter "Advance (3.e5)"]

{The most common line you'll face. White grabs space. You develop your bishop before ...e6 locks it in, then hit the d4 pawn with ...c5.}
1. e4 c6 {Prepares ...d5 with support. Patience is your weapon.} 2. d4 d5 3. e5 Bf5 {The whole point of the Caro: the bishop gets out before ...e6.}
4. Nf3
( 4. Nc3 e6 5. g4 {White goes aggressive.} Bg6 6. Nge2 c5 7. h4 h5 {Don't let h5 trap your bishop. Answer it with ...h5 yourself.} )
( 4. Bd3 Bxd3 5. Qxd3 e6 6. Nf3 c5 {White traded off your "good" bishop. That's fine, and ...c5 is still the plan.} 7. O-O Nc6 )
( 4. c3 e6 5. Be2 c5 6. Nf3 Nc6 7. O-O cxd4 8. cxd4 Nge7 )
e6 5. Be2
( 5. Bd3 Bxd3 6. Qxd3 c5 7. O-O Nc6 {Bad bishops traded, pressure on d4.} )
c5 {Strike the base of the chain. ...c5 is your plan in every Advance line.} 6. O-O
( 6. Be3 cxd4 7. Nxd4 Ne7 8. O-O Nbc6 {Develop and challenge the d4 knight.} )
( 6. c3 Nc6 7. O-O cxd4 8. cxd4 Nge7 )
Nc6 7. c3 cxd4 8. cxd4 Nge7 {Plan ...Nc8-b6 or ...Ng6, and pressure d4. You're fine. Better than fine.}

[Chapter "Exchange and Panov"]

{White trades on d5. Symmetrical, calm, and you develop more easily. The Panov (4.c4) is sharper: White gets an isolated pawn and you play against it.}
1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. exd5 cxd5 4. Bd3
( 4. Nf3 Nf6 5. Bd3 Nc6 6. c3 Bg4 )
( 4. c4 {The Panov. White gets an isolated queen pawn.} Nf6 5. Nc3 e6 6. Nf3
  ( 6. Bg5 Be7 7. Nf3 O-O )
  Be7 7. cxd5 Nxd5 8. Bd3 Nc6 9. O-O O-O {Blockade d5, trade pieces, win the endgame. That's the deal.} )
Nc6 5. c3
( 5. Nf3 Bg4 6. c3 Nf6 )
Nf6 6. Bf4
( 6. Nf3 Bg4 7. O-O e6 8. Bf4 Bd6 {Trade the dark bishops. Comfortable for you.} )
Bg4 {Your bishop gets out with purpose.} 7. Nf3
( 7. Qb3 {Attacks b7 and d5.} Qd7 {Defends everything. Don't panic over b7.} 8. Nd2 e6 9. Ngf3 Bd6 )
e6 8. Nbd2 Bd6 9. Bxd6 Qxd6 {Equal and easy. Minority attack with ...b5-b4 later.}

[Chapter "Classical (3.Nc3 / 3.Nd2)"]

{The main line. You take on e4, then your bishop goes to f5 and gets chased to g6 and h7. Learn the dance once and it's yours forever.}
1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3
( 3. Nd2 dxe4 4. Nxe4 Bf5 {Same thing.} )
dxe4 4. Nxe4 Bf5 {Develop with tempo on the knight. Never play ...Ngf6 too early in the 4...Nd7 lines: 5.Qe2 Ngf6?? 6.Nd6# is a smothered mate.}
5. Ng3
( 5. Nc5 b6 6. Nb3 e6 {The knight wasted time.} )
Bg6 6. h4 {White wants to trap your bishop with h5.}
( 6. Nf3 Nd7 7. h4 h6 8. h5 Bh7 9. Bd3 Bxd3 10. Qxd3 e6 )
( 6. Bc4 e6 7. N1e2 Nf6 8. O-O Bd6 )
( 6. N1e2 Nf6 7. h4 h6 8. Nf4 Bh7 )
h6 {Gives the bishop a home on h7.} 7. Nf3 Nd7 {Stops Ne5 hitting g6.} 8. h5 Bh7 9. Bd3 Bxd3 10. Qxd3 e6 11. Bd2 Ngf6 12. O-O-O Be7 {Castle short, play ...c5 or ...Qc7, and you're solid as stone.}

[Chapter "Two Knights and other sidelines"]

{White avoids 2.d4. Pin the f3 knight, take the bishop pair away from them, and play ...e6 and ...Nf6.}
1. e4 c6 2. Nc3
( 2. Nf3 d5 3. Nc3
  ( 3. e5 Bf5 4. d4 e6 {The Advance by another route.} )
  Bg4 4. h3 Bxf3 5. Qxf3 e6 )
( 2. c4 d5 3. exd5 cxd5 4. cxd5 Nf6 5. Nc3 Nxd5 6. Nf3 Nc6 )
( 2. d3 d5 3. Nd2 e5 4. Ngf3 Bd6 5. g3 Nf6 6. Bg2 O-O 7. O-O {White is playing the King's Indian Attack. You have a lovely center.} )
( 2. Bc4 d5 3. exd5 cxd5 4. Bb3 Nf6 5. d4 Nc6 )
( 2. d4 d5 3. f3 {The Fantasy variation.} e6 4. Nc3 Bb4 {Pin and pressure e4.} )
d5 3. Nf3
( 3. d4 dxe4 4. Nxe4 Bf5 {Back in the Classical.} )
Bg4 {Pin the knight.} 4. h3
( 4. Be2 e6 5. d4 Nf6 6. e5 Nfd7 {French-like, but your bishop is already outside the chain.} )
Bxf3 5. Qxf3 e6 {White has the bishop pair. You have a rock-solid center.} 6. d4
( 6. d3 Nf6 7. Bd2 Nbd7 )
( 6. Be2 Nf6 7. O-O Nbd7 )
Nf6 7. Bd3 dxe4 8. Nxe4 Nxe4 9. Qxe4 Nd7 10. O-O Nf6 {Hit the queen, develop, castle. Solid.}
